# Analysis plan: sample-pca

Status: APPROVED 2026-11-10

## Goal
Perform a principal component analysis of the gene count matrix, with sample points coloured by condition, to describe the main axes of variation among the six samples.

## Inputs
Resolve `counts_gene` and `design` for `rnaseq_bulk` through `_system/resolve_artifact.py` again at execution time. Current paths below are relative to the project root; inputs remain read-only.

| Artifact type | Resolved from | Path |
|---|---|---|
| counts_gene | 01_nfcore-rnaseq-wrapper (native) | 02_bioinformatics/rnaseq_bulk/01_nfcore-rnaseq-wrapper/run/results/star_salmon/salmon.merged.gene_counts_length_scaled.tsv |
| design | 01_prepare_samplesheets (native) | 01_samplesheets/rnaseq_bulk_design.csv |

The design contains A1–A3 (untreated) and B1–B3 (treated). The count table has gene_id and gene_name annotation columns followed by sample columns; length-scaled Salmon counts can be fractional and will not be rounded.

## Method
1. After approval, implement `scripts/sample_pca.py` and `scripts/submit.sh`. Run all data processing and plotting in a batch job using Python in `gars-bio`, with pandas, NumPy and Matplotlib (Agg backend). Check required imports before processing; if unavailable, report the failure without installing packages. Record package versions, input paths, supplying stages and SHA-256 checksums in the report.
2. Resolve both inputs using the resolver and require successful resolution and existing files. Parse the TSV count table and CSV design. Use gene_id as the feature identifier; exclude gene_name from numeric data. Require unique, nonempty gene IDs and sample IDs, finite nonnegative numeric counts, a nonempty condition for every sample, and exact equality between count sample columns and design sample IDs. Reorder columns to design order; do not silently rename or drop samples. Fail on invalid inputs or zero sample library totals.
3. Normalize each sample to counts per million (CPM), using its total across all input genes. Retain genes with CPM >= 1 in at least 3 of the 6 samples. Transform retained values as log2(CPM + 1); remove genes with zero variance across samples. Use all remaining genes, with no selection based on condition or differential expression. Record gene counts before and after each filter. Fail if fewer than two genes or fewer than two nonzero principal components remain.
4. Transpose to samples by genes and centre each gene across samples. Do not scale genes to unit variance or regress out condition/batch. Compute deterministic full singular value decomposition with `numpy.linalg.svd(full_matrices=False)`. Sample scores are U times singular values; explained variance is singular value squared divided by (n_samples - 1), and variance fractions use the total variance of all components. Fix each component's sign by making its loading with greatest absolute magnitude positive (first gene in input order breaks ties). Export all n_samples - 1 components, including any zero-variance trailing components.
5. Plot PC1 against PC2, one labelled point per sample, coloured by condition (untreated: #0072B2; treated: #D55E00). Include a condition legend and explained-variance percentages in both axis labels. Save matching PNG (300 dpi) and PDF versions. Report the method, retained dimensions and variance percentages descriptively; no hypothesis testing, automatic outlier exclusion or causal interpretation is planned.
6. Save the declared tables and report under results/, with commands and batch logs under scripts/ and logs/. On successful batch completion, run `stage03_analysis.py verify` and append its returned history entry verbatim to the project HISTORY.md. Only the helper writes OUTPUTS.tsv and completion STATUS. A changed method requires a new plan.

